       llddaappuurrll      [--aa attrs]      [--bb _s_e_a_r_c_h_b_a_s_e]     [--ee [!!]_e_x_t[==_e_x_t_p_a_r_a_m]]
       [--EE [!!]_e_x_t[==_e_x_t_p_a_r_a_m]] [--ff _f_i_l_t_e_r] [--HH _l_d_a_p_u_r_i] [--hh _l_d_a_p_h_o_s_t] [--pp _l_d_a_p_‐
       _p_o_r_t] [--ss {bbaassee|oonnee|ssuubb|cchhiillddrreenn}] [--SS _s_c_h_e_m_e]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       _l_d_a_p_u_r_l is a command that allows one to  either  compose  or  decompose
       LDAP URIs.

       When invoked with the --HH option, llddaappuurrll extracts the components of the
       _l_d_a_p_u_r_i  option argument, unescaping hex‐escaped chars as required.  It
       basically acts as a frontend to the llddaapp__uurrll__ppaarrssee(3) call.  Otherwise,
       it builds an LDAP URI based on the components passed with the appropri‐
       ate options, performing the inverse operation.  Option --HH is incompati‐
       ble with options --aa, --bb, --EE, --ff, --HH, --hh, --pp, --SS, and --ss.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --aa _a_t_t_r_s
              Set a comma‐separated list of attribute selectors.

       --bb _s_e_a_r_c_h_b_a_s_e
              Set the _s_e_a_r_c_h_b_a_s_e.

       --ee [!!]_e_x_t[==_e_x_t_p_a_r_a_m]

              Specify general extensions with --ee ´!!´ indicates criticality.

              General extensions:
                [!]assert=<filter>    (an RFC 4515 Filter)
                !authzid=<authzid>    ("dn:<dn>" or "u:<user>")
                [!]bauthzid           (RFC 3829 authzid control)
                [!]chaining[=<resolve>[/<cont>]]
                [!]manageDSAit
                [!]noop
                ppolicy
                [!]postread[=<attrs>] (a comma‐separated attribute list)
                [!]preread[=<attrs>]  (a comma‐separated attribute list)
                [!]relax
                sessiontracking
                abandon,cancel,ignore (SIGINT sends abandon/cancel,
                or ignores response; if critical, doesn’t wait for SIGINT.
                not really controls)


       --EE [!!]_e_x_t[==_e_x_t_p_a_r_a_m]
              Set URL extensions; incompatible with --HH.

       --ff _f_i_l_t_e_r
              Set the URL filter.  No particular check on conformity with  RFC
              4515  LDAP filters is performed, but the value is hex‐escaped as
              required.

       --HH _l_d_a_p_u_r_i
              Specify URI to be exploded.

       --hh _l_d_a_p_h_o_s_t
              Set the host.

       --pp _l_d_a_p_p_o_r_t
              Set the TCP port.

       --SS _s_c_h_e_m_e
              Set the URL scheme.  Defaults for other fields,  like  _l_d_a_p_p_o_r_t,
              may depend on the value of _s_c_h_e_m_e.

       --ss {bbaassee|oonnee|ssuubb|cchhiillddrreenn}
              Specify  the scope of the search to be one of bbaassee, oonnee, ssuubb, or
              cchhiillddrreenn to specify a base object, one‐level, subtree, or  chil‐
              dren search.  The default is ssuubb.  Note: cchhiillddrreenn scope requires
              LDAPv3 subordinate feature extension.


OOUUTTPPUUTT FFOORRMMAATT
       If  the --HH option is used, the _l_d_a_p_u_r_i supplied is exploded in its com‐
       ponents, which are printed to standard output in an LDIF‐like form.

       Otherwise, the URI built using the values passed with the other options
       is printed to standard output.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEE
       The following command:

           ldapurl -h ldap.example.com -b dc=example,dc=com -s sub -f "(cn=Some One)"

       returns

           ldap://ldap.example.com:389/dc=example,dc=com??sub?(cn=Some%20One)

       The command:

           ldapurl -H ldap://ldap.example.com:389/dc=example,dc=com??sub?(cn=Some%20One)

       returns

           scheme: ldap
           host: ldap.example.com
           port: 389
           dn: dc=example,dc=com
           scope: sub
           filter: (cn=Some One)

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
       Exit status is zero if no errors occur.  Errors result  in  a  non‐zero
       exit status and a diagnostic message being written to standard error.

